# 168 · One message bus: NATS JetStream between control, rigs and agents, with fast storage and retrieval

**Status:** `READY` · **Version:** [v0.1.1](../roadmap.md#v011) · **Depends on:** [167](_completed/167-how-the-parts-talk.goal)

## What

NATS with JetStream becomes the channel between Thermite's systems, in place of today's many HTTP polling loops: events, commands and their answers, reports, live views, past research and the dashboard's live updates.

1. **A design first,** from the review of goal [167](_completed/167-how-the-parts-talk.goal): subjects and streams for each channel, what stays in SQLite, the accounts and permissions of each part, how rigs reach it (on one machine, over SSH, on a cluster, and on RunPod, where a rig dials out), and the order of the move.
2. **NATS in every deployment,** starting with the one-machine compose file: its image, its storage, its health check, and what a user configures.
3. **The channels move to it,** one at a time, with the system working at every step.
4. **Storage and retrieval made fast:** control's pages read views kept up to date as events arrive, instead of folding every event of a rig on every request.
5. **API and MCP coverage:** the state of the bus (streams, consumers and their lag, each rig's connection) through REST, MCP and the command line, and on the dashboard.

## Why

The owner, 2026-10-26: "I want us to optimize the data storage and retrieval and message bus between systems. We have so many moving parts here its probably time to throw in nats.io jetstream and have that as part of single machine podman compose, and using it as the communication channel between all our systems. With solid API and MCP coverage."

The audit found the cost this removes: control rebuilds each rig's view from every event on every request (about 0.55 s for each rig at 100,000 events), and the parts talk through polling loops with no shared contract ([the clean-up guide](../code-clean-up-guide.md), themes T2 and T12).

## Constraints

- **The design invariants hold.** The ledger stays the record ("Nothing is destroyed"); commands stay signed and checked by the harness; agents never reach the judge's state.
- **The network rules** in [decisions.md](../decisions.md) change only by the owner's decision, recorded there.
- **New dependencies and downloads** (the NATS server's image, the client libraries) come with the owner's yes, and each is credited in [CREDITS.md](../../../CREDITS.md).
- **v0.1.1 may break compatibility with v0.1.0** (owner, 2026-10-26).
- **Docs follow the code,** in the same commit.

## Done when

- [ ] The design is in this goal, with the owner's decisions.
- [ ] NATS runs in the compose stack and in every other deployment, with a health check, and the docs say how to run it.
- [ ] Every channel the design moves runs over NATS, and the polling it replaces is gone.
- [ ] Control's pages read views kept up to date as events arrive, measured before and after at 100,000 events.
- [ ] REST, MCP, the command line and the dashboard show the bus's state; the reference pages list them.
- [ ] The fast checks, the docs checks and the acceptance checks for every changed part pass.

## What the review found

The audit's lane 11 (`.internal/reviews/v0.1.1-audit/11-message-bus.md`) evaluated NATS JetStream for this goal on 2026-10-26, from NATS's own documentation and measurements of Thermite's code. It installed and ran nothing.

- **Its recommendation:** build the first part below over HTTP now, and decide NATS afterwards, with that part's numbers and goal [169](169-agents-in-their-terminal.goal)'s needs. If NATS is chosen, use core NATS with nothing in it that is not also in SQLite or the ledger, so it needs no backup of its own.
- **The measured cost is not the transport.** Control takes in about 52,000 events a second; a page of 1,000 events is about 0.5 MB and takes 3.3 ms to sign and 2.8 ms to verify. The cost is the refold of every event for each view, which a bus does not change. What polling costs is delay: up to 5 s for commands, 60 s for reports, 30 s for model routes and 180 s for past research.
- **One channel can't be reached.** Kits travel as git over HTTP, large files stay on HTTP, and the harness stays off any shared service. A RunPod rig does not dial out: control connects to the pod through RunPod's proxy, which is why pull mode exists, so a bus there needs control exposed or a listener in the pod. NATS would be a second channel beside HTTPS.
- **JetStream would keep a second copy** of reports and commands, with its own retention and backups, which can drift from the records.
- **More to secure and to test:** a server, its ports and a second way to authenticate; the Node library for NATS's auth callout is a pre-release; every acceptance check would start a NATS server.

## Design

The owner chose this design on 2026-10-26, from a drawing of it: JetStream as the event log, the delivery and the queues between control, the rigs' supervisors and the dashboard. The review's first part over HTTP is folded into it where it still applies (the link's contract, views kept up to date).

**Who connects**

- **Control** runs the NATS server, with JetStream.
- **A rig on a machine or a cluster:** its supervisor connects to control's NATS server on NATS's own port, over TLS, with a key the rig made and registered over today's HTTPS call (the owner, 2026-10-27).
- **A rig on RunPod:** a small NATS server in the pod accepts one connection only: control's own server, which dials it as a leaf node over TLS, with keys checked both ways. Control mints the keys at launch and learns the pod's address from RunPod's API, so control never needs to be public. It replaces pull mode's tunnel.
- **The dashboard** reads its views through control (question 4).

**Off the bus, on purpose**

- **The harness** keeps its SQLite ledger: the judge's leases and fencing, and the compare-and-set promotion, stay there. It talks to its supervisor over HTTP, as today. The supervisor publishes the harness's signed events and delivers its commands.
- **The agents** never hold a bus credential; the harness acts for them.
- **Kits and large files** stay on git and HTTP.

**Subjects** follow goal [170](170-one-hierarchy.goal)'s hierarchy; the rig and the ledger travel in headers, never in a run's subject.

| Subject | Stream or bucket | Truth | Kept |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `thermite.run.<run>.ev.<kind>`, `thermite.run.<run>.attempt.<attempt>.<kind>`, `thermite.run.<run>.trial.<trial>.<kind>` | `EVENTS`, a record | the rig's ledger | for good; deletes and purges refused |
| `thermite.rig.<rig>.ev.<kind>` | `RIG_EVENTS`, a record | the rig's ledger | for good |
| `thermite.run.<run>.queue.<op>` | `EVENTS`, and a key-value bucket of each experiment's state | the rig's ledger | for good; the bucket holds the latest, changed by compare-and-set |
| `thermite.rig.<rig>.cmd` | `COMMANDS`, a work queue | control signs and keeps each; the rig's ledger keeps its outcome | until the rig acks |
| `thermite.run.<run>.live.<source>` | `LIVE`, a view | none | by count and age |
| `thermite.rig.<rig>.config` | a key-value bucket | control's store | the latest; secrets sealed to the rig's key |
| `thermite.campaign.<campaign>.<kind>` | `CAMPAIGNS`, a record | control's store | for good |
| `KB_FILES` | an object store: each file of the knowledge base by its SHA-256, put before its `kb_item` event (goal [175](175-knowledge-base.goal)) | the rig's ledger; then control's knowledge base | for good |
| `thermite.kb.search`, `thermite.kb.read` | request and reply, not kept | control's knowledge base | not kept |

Control's own SQLite copies of the rigs' events and of its commands go: JetStream holds control's one copy, and control's pages read views that consumers keep up to date. JetStream snapshots are its backup.

**Who may do what.** Only control and the rigs' supervisors hold bus credentials. A supervisor publishes only its own rig's subjects and its runs' (control also checks that the run registry gives each run to that rig), takes its own rig's commands, reads its rig's config, puts its runs' files into `KB_FILES`, and asks control to search and read the collections its runs may read. Control publishes commands, each signed, config and campaigns, answers searches of the knowledge base, and reads everything. The owner and operators act through control, which signs what they may issue. Every record a rig publishes keeps the harness's signature inside it, so a bus credential alone can forge nothing.

**The experiment queue** stays as it is inside the rig: its truth is the harness's ledger, and every change is one transaction and one event. The owner changes it by signed commands; the planner accepts, adds, moves, drops and hands out (it gives each lane its experiment when the lane's trial starts; workers never pick); the teachers and the auditor add and move; workers propose; the judge adds ports. The bus changes only how the owner's changes arrive and how the queue's events leave.

**Still needed, whatever the transport:** the link's contract in `core/link/` (one module for each message type: its subject, schema, byte limit and signer), views kept up to date as events arrive, and the link's state in REST, MCP and `/metrics`.

**The steps,** each green on its own:

0. **A spike in compose:** the `nats` service with JetStream; the supervisor publishes its events to `EVENTS`; control consumes and folds them. Measured against today's polling.
1. **Commands:** the `COMMANDS` work queue for each rig, acked by the supervisor, outcomes as events. The command poll goes.
2. **Live and the dashboard:** the `LIVE` stream, and the dashboard's push. The live poll goes.
3. **Config and the knowledge base:** the config bucket; the knowledge base's writes (`kb_item` events and `KB_FILES`) and its searches (`thermite.kb.search` and `.read`), as goal 175 designs them. The route, MCP and past-research polls go.
4. **Queues, and control's own copies:** the queue's subjects and bucket; control's tables of rig events and commands go.
5. **Every placement:** RunPod's leaf node, machines and clusters, the release bundle and every acceptance stack.
6. **Later, by decision:** the harness on a rig-local NATS, and the ledger moving toward JetStream.

## Decisions

- 2026-10-26: the audit's lane 11 evaluated NATS JetStream; it recommended a first part over HTTP and a decision on NATS after it.
- 2026-10-26, owner, after a drawing of the design above: "for jetstream direction, this looks great". JetStream is the event log, the delivery and the queues between control, the rigs' supervisors and the dashboard, with the harness and the agents off the bus at first, bridged by the supervisor ([decisions.md](../decisions.md#the-product)). This answers the review's questions on when to add NATS (now, as designed), on RunPod (a leaf node in the pod, dialed by control, in place of the tunnel), on what JetStream holds (records, the commands queue, the live view and key-value buckets, with control's own copies gone), on a report soon after each command (events now arrive as they happen) and on one configuration document for each rig (the config bucket).
- 2026-10-26, owner: input typed into an agent's terminal is signed, owner only: text, slash commands, skills and a fixed list of named keys, each a signed command recorded word for word with its outcome. Input travels as a command on the rig's commands stream.
- 2026-10-27, owner (goal [175](175-knowledge-base.goal)): the knowledge base lives on control, and its writes arrive as durable events on NATS. A write is a `kb_item` event in `EVENTS` with its file in the `KB_FILES` object store; searches go to control by request and reply. The `KB` stream from control to the rigs goes: no rig keeps a copy of the knowledge base.
- 2026-10-27 23:42 UTC, owner, the seven open questions, asked as goal 172's step 5 began:
  1. A rig on a machine or a cluster reaches control's NATS server on NATS's own port, with TLS: a second port to open and a certificate for it. The review had recommended WebSocket through the operator's HTTPS reverse proxy.
  2. Each supervisor makes its own key and registers it over today's HTTPS call; control writes the server's users file and reloads it. The secret never leaves the rig.
  3. Secrets travel sealed to each rig's own key, on every rig, and only when they change.
  4. The dashboard's push is Server-Sent Events from control first; a read-only NATS credential for each session may come later.
  5. The auditor and the review panel get a loop of their own, so a promotion never waits for a teacher's round.
  6. Every reader of a signed batch keeps what it knows and counts the rest, written once in `core` as the rule for every subject.
  7. The supervisor's figures count messages for each channel and subject.

## Open questions

None: the owner answered the seven on 2026-10-27 (see Decisions).

The question of an assignment that expires while its models download is goal [171](_completed/171-bugs-on-the-wire.goal)'s item 5.

## Progress

- 2026-10-26 21:03 UTC: Goal written from the owner's words. Lanes 10 and 11 of the audit review the channels and the bus.
- 2026-10-26 22:33 UTC: The owner chose the JetStream design above. Next: the spike in compose (step 0).
- 2026-10-26 23:34 UTC: The design and the owner's decisions are in; the goal is READY. Building waits for the owner's word: the owner set this phase to documentation and planning only.
- 2026-10-27 01:42 UTC: The knowledge base's subjects follow goal 175's design: `kb_item` events and `KB_FILES` for writes, `thermite.kb.search` and `.read` for searches; the `KB` stream to the rigs goes.
- 2026-10-27 23:54 UTC: Started by goal 172's step 5; the owner answered the seven questions (Decisions). The plan for steps 0 and 1:
  - **What it takes:** the NATS server's image in compose, pinned by digest, with JetStream and a volume; `@nats-io/transport-node` 3.4.0, `@nats-io/jetstream` 3.4.0 and `@nats-io/nkeys` 2.0.3 (all pre-approved, at gate B), each in CREDITS.md.
  - **Step 0, the link's first contract:** `core/link/report.ts` names the subject (`thermite.rig.<rig>.report`), the schema (the signed report the harness makes today), its byte limit (the server's largest payload, which the supervisor's pages keep under) and its signer (the rig's key). A message is one signed report: the harness's signature stays inside it, and control reads it with today's ingest checks, keeping what it knows (question 6). The design's per-run subjects follow when T2 gives each event its own signer.
  - **Step 0, the move:** with `THERMITE_NATS_URL` set, the supervisor publishes each page to JetStream (its message id the ledger and cursor, so a page sent twice is kept once) and moves its cursor on the server's ack; control folds the stream with a durable consumer through the same ingest code as `POST /v1/rigs/<rig>/ingest`, which stays for rigs not yet on the bus. Measured in the compose smoke: the time from an event to control's view, on the bus and by polling.
  - **Step 1, commands:** a `COMMANDS` work queue with one subject for each rig. Control publishes each signed command there as well as recording it; the supervisor takes its rig's subject, delivers each command to its harness, acks it, and reports the outcome as today. Each rig's key (question 2) limits it to its own subjects. The command poll goes in compose; every placement follows in step 5, with TLS on NATS's own port (question 1).
- 2026-10-28 01:21 UTC: Step 0 is built, opt in: the compose file's `nats` service (profile `bus`, the server's image pinned by digest, on control's network only, its store on a volume) and `THERMITE_NATS_URL` for control and the supervisor. The link's first contract is `core/link/report.ts` (the `REPORTS` stream, `thermite.rig.<rig>.report`, kept for good, 8 MiB at most, a message id of the ledger, the cursor and the page's end), the one client of the bus is `common/bus.ts`, and control takes a report from the bus or from HTTP through one function (`control/ingest.ts`). Against a real server, 2,500 events in 3 pages were in control's store about 86 ms after the supervisor's round began; the compose smoke prints the delay with and without the bus. Open, for step 1: a page control refuses on the bus (a key that waits for the owner, a version refused) is not sent again, so the ledger's later pages are refused as a gap until control's answer to the supervisor names control's cursor and the supervisor publishes again from there, as the HTTP route's 409 does today; and a rig has no credential of its own on the bus yet.

